  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sida Fair Finance Bolivia 24 27 Oxfam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Priority sectors for Finanzas Justas y Responsables &#x2013; Bolivia are infrastructural projects, agribusiness, and extractive industries(gold mining). Target FIs are international investors and banks, including the IADB. The coalition focusses specifically on women and youth&#x2019;s rights. Their main objectives are to: (1) strengthen the coalition through capacity building on investigation and advocacy on financial investments and institutions; (2) generate knowledge to promote advocacy; and (3) contribute to guarantee indigenous,citizens, women, and environmental rights.
Capacity building will focus on CSOs, community organizations and the financial and business entities themselves. A training program will be developed that includes, among others, the mapping of the national financial system and the characteristics of productive credits, the importance of safeguards and their implementation, as well as international regulations and the existing regulatory framework in Bolivia. Case studies will include an intersectional approach on the impacts offinancing for women and/or youth.
Fair Finance Bolivia seeks the following results: (1) Critical mass of institutions, community actors and people who have the skills and abilities to monitor and influence financial entities and financial flows that have an impact in Bolivia. (2) The issue of public and responsible finance is incorporated into the agendas of state, financial, civil society, and community actors. (3) Government has policy and regulatory instruments topromote responsible fair finance.
Case studies will identify impacts of financial flows and investments on land use and territorial planning. FF Bolivia will also conduct a Policy Assessment on Multilateral Development Bank(s), probably the IADB and/or the CAF92.
Finanzas Justas y Responsables Bolivia members include: Oxfam in Bolivia, the Centre of Studies for Labour and Agrarian Development (CEDLA), Foundation for the Development of the National System of Protected Areas (FUNDESNAP), Bolivian Forum on Environment and</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="23">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Intermon Oxfam Bolivia</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam has been working in Bolivia since 1988, through different local partners, including civil society organizations, associations, networks and different levels of the Bolivian Government, based on complementarity and mutual respect.

Our work in Bolivia focuses on reducing vulnerability through the sustainable exploitation of natural resources, political activism and increased productivity. We advocate for the creation of a more just society through our research and mobilisation of civil society andwe carry out long-term development projects that aim to reduce risk and increase resilience.

Priority social and geographical sectors

We strengthen the influencing capacities of indigenous populations, agricultural workers, and youth to promote public, redistributive, efficient, and transparent policies.
We work with women and mixed-sex youth groups aiming to reduce violence, to empower them and promote better economic opportunities.
We support land management, access to natural resources, risk reduction and adaptation of indigenous and rural communities.
In Lowlands we provide support so that indigenous populations, communities, and farmers can overcome their vulnerability, exclusion, and the discrimination they face.
We work with women, youth,and on indigenous population participation, adaptation,land management, and risk reduction in the main cities of Bolivia (La Paz, El Alto, Cochabamba, and Santa Cruz) as well as with thecities with rapid population growth located in the Amazons (Trinidad and Riberalta)..</narrative>
  </participating-org>
